\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Singular eliminated directions and changed reference volume}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Use the orthogonal spaces and components of
[Gaussian elimination](fdip-000D), but assume only #{Q=Q^\dagger\geq0}.
Then #{C\geq0}. If #{N=\ker C\ne0}, every full fiber integral is
infinite:
##{\int_Y e^{-h_E(J(x,y),QJ(x,y))/2}\,d\lambda_Y(y)=\infty.}
Replacing #{C^{-1}} by a pseudoinverse does not change that integral.}
\proof{
\p{For #{z\in N}, #{x\in X} and every real #{r}, positivity gives
##{0\leq h_X(x,Ax)+2r h_X(x,Bz).}
An affine real function bounded below for all #{r} has slope zero.
Thus #{h_X(x,Bz)=0} for every #{x}; taking #{x=Bz} gives #{Bz=0}.
Adjointness consequently gives #{B^\dagger X\subseteq N^\perp}.
The weight at #{J(x,z+y_0)} is independent of #{z\in N}.}
\p{Put #{Y_0=N^\perp}. Choose a compact orthonormal unit box
#{D\subseteq Y_0} of positive finite induced volume. If #{Y_0=0},
use its singleton of mass one. For fixed #{x}, the continuous
strictly positive weight has a minimum #{\epsilon_x>0} on #{D}.
With #{k=\dim N>0}, integrate over the product of #{D} and the
orthonormal box #{[-R,R]^k\subseteq N}. [Product volume](fdip-002Q)
gives the lower bound #{\epsilon_x\lambda_{Y_0}(D)(2R)^k}.
It tends to infinity. This proves divergence without multiplying
an unexplained infinite constant by an integral.}
}
\p{For example, on #{X=Y=\mathbb R}, #{Q(x,y)=(x,0)} has weight
#{e^{-x^2/2}}, independent of #{y}. Thus
#{e^{-x^2/2}\int_{\mathbb R}dy=\infty} for every #{x}.}

\p{A different model integrates only over #{Y_0} with its
[induced Euclidean volume](fdip-002M). Let #{j_0:Y_0\to Y}
and #{p_0:Y\to Y_0} be orthogonal inclusion and projection.
The restriction #{C_0=C|_{Y_0}} is positive definite, and the
[Moore-Penrose construction](fdip-0032) is
##{C^+=j_0C_0^{-1}p_0.}
For #{r=\dim Y_0=\operatorname{rank}C}, put
##{\begin{aligned}
S_0&=A-BC^+B^\dagger,\\
c_0&=\frac{(2\pi)^{r/2}}{\sqrt{\operatorname{pdet}C}}.
\end{aligned}}
Then #{S_0\geq0} and
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_{Y_0}e^{-h_E(J(x,j_0y),QJ(x,j_0y))/2}\,d\lambda_{Y_0}(y)\\
&\qquad=c_0e^{-h_X(x,S_0x)/2}.
\end{aligned}}
A normalized law on the full retained #{X} exists exactly when
#{S_0>0}.}
\proof{
\p{Self-adjointness makes #{Y_0} invariant under #{C}. Its
[spectral decomposition](fdip-002R) shows that #{Y_0} is the sum of
positive eigenspaces; hence #{C_0>0}. Its determinant is
#{\operatorname{pdet}C}, including the empty product one.
Since #{B^\dagger X\subseteq Y_0}, put
#{K_0=C_0^{-1}p_0B^\dagger}. The same expansion as in square
completion gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&h_E(J(x,j_0y),QJ(x,j_0y))\\
&\qquad=h_X(x,S_0x)\\
&\qquad\quad+h_{Y_0}(y+K_0x,C_0(y+K_0x)).
\end{aligned}}
Testing #{y=-K_0x} proves #{S_0\geq0}. Translation invariance on
#{Y_0} and [positive normalization](fdip-002T) give the stated
changed-space integral. If #{S_0>0}, a further Gaussian integral
on #{X} is finite. If #{S_0} has a nonzero kernel, the same box
argument proves divergence there. A finite-dimensional positive
semidefinite self-adjoint map without a kernel is positive definite,
so these exhaust the possibilities.}
\p{In rank zero, #{C=0}, #{B=0}, #{Y_0=0}, #{C^+=0},
#{\operatorname{pdet}C=1}, #{c_0=1} and #{S_0=A}. Integration over
#{Y_0} is evaluation on its unit-mass singleton. When #{X=0},
its retained normalizer is one. These conventions cover the zero
spaces without restricting ambient Lebesgue measure to a lower-dimensional
subspace.}
}
\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.5, p. 651}{boyd2004convex} treats a singular
positive-semidefinite pivot, its range condition and pseudoinverse
quadratic minimum. Exchanging the pivot gives the algebra used here.
The divergence and induced-volume calculation above are additional
arguments. The [constrained Gaussian construction](fdip-000C)
likewise changes the space and measure. Neither a general indefinite
pivot nor a regularized value for the original divergent fiber is asserted.}
